Practical 8: Decision Tree Based ID3 Algorithm
Aim: Demonstrate an entropy-based decision tree and classify a new sample.

Dataset: Real Iris dataset from Scikit-learn.

Technical note: Scikit-learn supports entropy-based decision trees, but it does not implement the original categorical ID3 algorithm exactly. This experiment demonstrates the ID3 information-gain principle on a real dataset.
Complete Python program


import pandas as pd
from sklearn.datasets import load_iris
from sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split
from sklearn.tree import DecisionTreeClassifier, export_text
from sklearn.metrics import accuracy_score

# Load the real Iris dataset
iris = load_iris(as_frame=True)

X = iris.data
y = iris.target

# Split the dataset
X_train, X_test, y_train, y_test = train_test_split(
    X, y,
    test_size=0.30,
    random_state=42,
    stratify=y
)

# Create an entropy-based decision tree
id3_model = DecisionTreeClassifier(
    criterion="entropy",
    random_state=42
)

# Train the model
id3_model.fit(X_train, y_train)

# Predict test samples
predictions = id3_model.predict(X_test)

# Calculate accuracy
accuracy = accuracy_score(y_test, predictions)

print("Decision Tree Using Entropy")
print("Accuracy:", accuracy)
print("Accuracy Percentage:", round(accuracy * 100, 2), "%")

# Display the learned decision rules
print("\nDecision Tree Rules:")
print(
    export_text(
        id3_model,
        feature_names=list(X.columns),
        max_depth=3
    )
)

# Classify a new sample
new_sample = pd.DataFrame([{
    "sepal length (cm)": 5.1,
    "sepal width (cm)": 3.5,
    "petal length (cm)": 1.4,
    "petal width (cm)": 0.2
}])

prediction = id3_model.predict(new_sample)[0]

print("New Sample:", new_sample.iloc[0].to_dict())
print("Predicted Class:", iris.target_names[prediction])

The program prints the complete learned rules up to depth 3. The prediction for the supplied measurements is setosa.

Result: An entropy-based decision tree was trained on the Iris dataset and used to classify a new sample.

